"**Shakespeare plays that mention or allude to pears**\n\n| Play | How pears are referenced | Source |\n|------|--------------------------|--------|\n| *All\u2019s\u202fWell That Ends\u202fWell* |\u2013 \u201cyour virginity \u2026 is like one of our French **withered pears**\u201d (Act\u202f1, Scene\u202f1).  <br>\u2013 In a descriptive list of plants in the play, **pear** is named alongside grapes, dates, and pomegranates. | https://www.folger.edu/explore/shakespeares-works/alls-well-that-ends-well/read/1/1/  <br>https://www.csgtour.org/alls-well-transcript |\n| *Romeo and\u202fJuliet* |\u2013 Mercutio\u2019s line: \u201cO that she were an open\u2011arse and thou a **pop'rin pear**\u201d (Act\u202f2, Scene\u202f1). | https://myshakespeare.com/romeo-and-juliet/act-2-scene-1-popup-note-index-item-o-she-were-open-arse-and-thou-popprin-pear |\n| *The Merry\u202fWives of\u202fWindsor* |\u2013 Physical allusion: \u201cI would make you \u2026 till I were **as crest\u2011fallen as a dried pear**\u201d (Act\u202f4, Scene\u202f5). | https://shakespeare.mit.edu/merry_wives/full.html |"